    answer: 'The funeral prayer (salat al-janazah) is prayed standing, with four takbirs. There is no ruku\' (bowing) and no sujud (prostration), no adhan and no iqamah: the prayer is takbirs, recitation and du\'a, then the salam. It is a communal duty (fard kifayah): when enough Muslims pray it, the rest are relieved.\n\nBefore you begin: be in wudu and face the qibla. The body lies in front of the imam and the people stand behind him in rows. The Prophet ﷺ stood level with the middle of a woman\'s body when he prayed over her (Sahih al-Bukhari 1331); where the imam stands for a man differs between the schools.\n\nThe steps:\n1. Make the intention in your heart, then say the first takbir, "Allahu Akbar".\n2. After the first takbir: the Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools recite al-Fatihah here, and Ibn Abbas did and said it is the Sunnah (Sahih al-Bukhari 1335). The Hanafi school recites the opening praise (thana\') instead.\n3. Second takbir, then send blessings on the Prophet ﷺ, as in the last sitting of every prayer.\n4. Third takbir, then make du\'a for the deceased and for the Muslims. The Prophet\'s du\'a, as \'Awf ibn Malik remembered it, begins: Allahumma-ghfir lahu, warhamhu, wa \'afihi, wa\'fu \'anhu. In meaning: O Allah, forgive him, have mercy on him, give him peace and pardon him, honour his resting place and make it wide, wash him clean of his faults, give him a better home and family, admit him to Paradise and protect him from the trial of the grave and the Fire (Sahih Muslim 963).\n5. Fourth takbir. The Hanafi school then pauses briefly and gives the salam; the Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools make a short du\'a first.\n6. Give the salam to the right (many scholars also to the left).\n\nFour takbirs is what the Prophet ﷺ said when he prayed over the Negus (Sahih al-Bukhari 1245). One companion reported that the Prophet once said five (Sahih Muslim 957); the schools follow four.\n\nRecitation after each takbir differs between the schools, so follow your own school or ask your imam. These school details are the standard teaching of the schools and are waiting for scholar review.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1331, 1335, 1245; Sahih Muslim 963, 957.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-janazah-prayer]',
