    answer: 'The Eid prayer (for Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha) is two rak\'ahs prayed in congregation, usually in an open ground or large masjid, after the sun has risen about a spear\'s length. There is no adhan and no iqamah.\n\nHow it goes:\n1. Make the intention and say the opening takbir.\n2. Extra takbirs: the Maliki, Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools say seven takbirs in the first rak\'ah (after the opening) and five in the second, raising the hands with each. The Hanafi school says three extra takbirs in each rak\'ah.\n3. Recite al-Fatihah and a surah, then finish each rak\'ah as in any prayer.\n4. After the salam, the imam gives the khutbah. The Prophet ﷺ prayed first and gave the khutbah after (Sahih al-Bukhari 957).\n\nRuling: the Hanafi school calls it wajib, the Maliki and Shafi\'i schools a strongly recommended sunnah, and the Hanbali school a communal duty (fard kifayah). Women are encouraged to attend; those who are menstruating stay out of the prayer area but share in the du\'a.\n\nBefore the Eid al-Fitr prayer it is a sunnah to eat some dates: the Prophet ﷺ never went out on that day until he had eaten some (Sahih al-Bukhari 953). Zakat al-fitr is paid before people go out to the prayer (Sahih al-Bukhari 1503).\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 957, 953, 1503.',
