    answer: 'Three different things, often mixed up:\n1. Qada (making up). If you miss days of Ramadan for a valid reason (illness, travel, period, pregnancy), fast the same number of days before the next Ramadan comes, one day for each day missed. They need not be in a row. Allah says: "an equal number of days [are to be made up]" (Qur\'an 2:184). If you are late by years, still make them up, and ask a scholar whether anything extra is due (the Hanafi school says nothing extra).\n2. Fidyah (ransom). For someone who cannot fast and cannot make it up (a very old person, a chronic illness): feed one poor person for each day missed (Qur\'an 2:184). The amount is a meal or its value; the schools set it differently (about half a sa\' of wheat, around 1.6 kg, in the Hanafi school; a mudd, about 750 g of staple food, in the others). Ask your masjid for the local figure.\n3. Kaffarah (penalty). For deliberately breaking a Ramadan fast by intercourse (and, in the Hanafi and Maliki schools, by eating or drinking on purpose): free a slave, or if not possible fast two months in a row, or if not possible feed sixty poor people (Sahih al-Bukhari 1936). On top of it, make up the day.\n\nIf you broke a fast on purpose in some other way, repent sincerely, make the day up and ask a scholar about the penalty for your school.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:184; Sahih al-Bukhari 1936.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-fasting]',
