    answer: 'Suhoor (the pre-dawn meal) and iftar (the meal that ends the fast) are both sunnah.\n\nSuhoor: eat before dawn; the Prophet ﷺ said: take suhoor, for there is a blessing in it (Sahih al-Bukhari 1923). Eat and drink until the white thread of dawn appears clearly from the black thread of night (Qur\'an 2:187); stop when the Fajr time begins.\n\nIftar: break the fast at sunset, without delay. The Prophet ﷺ said the people will keep to the right path as long as they hasten to break the fast (Sahih al-Bukhari 1957). His practice was to break his fast with fresh dates before praying, then dried dates, and if there were none, a few sips of water (Sunan Abu Dawud 2356, hasan sahih per al-Albani).\n\nThe du\'a: the Prophet ﷺ is reported to have said when breaking the fast: "Dhahaba az-zama\'u wabtallatil-\'urooqu wa thabatal-ajru in sha\' Allah" (the thirst has gone, the veins are moist, and the reward is confirmed, if Allah wills) (Sunan Abu Dawud 2357, hasan per al-Albani).\n\nThe intention is in the heart. It is enough to decide the night before that you will fast tomorrow (some schools require the night-before intention for a fard fast; the Hanafi school accepts it up to before midday for Ramadan). No words need to be said aloud.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1923, 1957; Qur\'an 2:187; Sunan Abu Dawud 2356, hasan sahih per al-Albani; Sunan Abu Dawud 2357, hasan per al-Albani.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-fasting]',
