    answer: 'Zakat al-fitr (also called fitrah) is a charity every Muslim gives at the end of Ramadan, so that the poor can celebrate Eid too, and it also purifies the fast.\n\nWho pays: every Muslim who has food beyond their needs for the day of Eid, for themselves and for those they support, including children.\n\nHow much: the Prophet ﷺ fixed it at one sa\' of dates or barley per person (Sahih al-Bukhari 1503). A sa\' is about 2.5 to 3 kg of staple food. Many people give its money value, and the Hanafi school allows this; other schools prefer food. The Hanafi school accepts half a sa\' of wheat, as the people did in Madinah (Sahih al-Bukhari 1511). Your local masjid announces the yearly amount in money.\n\nWhen: before the Eid prayer; it may be paid a few days before Eid, and it is best to give it early so the poor can use it. If given after the prayer it is counted as ordinary charity.\n\nWho receives it: the poor and needy, as with zakat.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1503, 1511.\n[GO:/zakat]',
    topic: 'zakat fitr',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The size of the sa\' in kilograms, the money-value allowance per school, and when it may be paid are standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
