      r'\blesser pilgrimage\b',
      r'\bumrah (?:compulsory|obligatory|fard|mandatory|required|sunnah)\b',
      r'\bis umrah (?:compulsory|obligatory|fard|mandatory|required|sunnah)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Umrah is the "lesser pilgrimage": a shorter, voluntary journey to the Kaaba that can be done at any time of the year. Hajj is the major pilgrimage with fixed days, obligatory once on every Muslim who is able.\n\nUmrah has four parts: (1) enter ihram at the miqat with the intention for Umrah, (2) circle the Kaaba seven times (tawaf), (3) walk between Safa and Marwah seven times (sa\'i), (4) shave or trim the hair (men shave or trim; women trim a fingertip\'s length). That is all; it takes a few hours.\n\nHajj includes similar acts (ihram, tawaf, sa\'i) but adds standing at Arafah, the night at Muzdalifah, stoning the pillars in Mina, and the sacrifice, over five or six fixed days in Dhul Hijjah.\n\nThe difference in ruling: Hajj is a pillar of Islam. Umrah is obligatory once for those able in the Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools, and a strongly recommended sunnah in the Hanafi and Maliki schools. The Prophet ﷺ said that one Umrah is an expiation for the sins between it and the previous one, and the reward of an accepted Hajj is nothing less than Paradise (Sahih al-Bukhari 1773). Umrah does not replace Hajj.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1773.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-umrah]\n[GO:/academy/hajj-umrah-pack]',
    topic: 'umrah',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The ruling on Umrah per school and the details of trimming are standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'riba-interest',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\briba\b',
      r'\binterest\b.*\b(?:haram|halal|islam|allowed|permissible|forbidden|prohibited|sin|bank|loan|mortgage|savings|account|credit)\b',
      r'\b(?:haram|halal|islam|allowed|permissible|forbidden|prohibited|sin)\b.*\binterest\b',
      r'\busury\b',
      r'\bbank interest\b',
      r'\binterest (?:on|from|in) (?:my )?(?:savings|bank|account|loan|deposit)\b',
      r'\bwhat to do with (?:bank )?interest\b',
      r'\bsavings account\b',
      r'\bcredit cards?\b',
      r'\bpersonal loans?\b',
      r'\bstudent loans?\b',
      r'\bcar loans?\b',
      r'\bis (?:it )?(?:haram|halal|ok|okay|allowed) to (?:take|get|have|use) (?:a )?(?:loan|credit)\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:mortgage|house loan|home loan)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Yes. Riba (interest, usury) is forbidden in Islam. Allah says: "Allah has permitted trade and has forbidden interest" (Qur\'an 2:275), and "fear Allah and give up what remains [due to you] of interest, if you should be believers" (Qur\'an 2:278-279). The Prophet ﷺ cursed the one who takes interest, the one who pays it, the one who records it, and the two witnesses (Sahih Muslim 1598).\n\nWhat this covers: interest on loans and credit cards, interest on savings accounts and fixed deposits, and any agreement where a loan carries an extra amount stated in advance. Paying a late fee that grows with time is interest too.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Borrow only when needed, from interest-free sources first (family, a qard hasan loan from a community fund, an employer).\n- Use a halal alternative: Islamic banks and finance firms (profit-sharing, lease-to-own, cost-plus sale), where available.\n- A credit card that you pay off in full every month, so no interest is ever charged, is considered acceptable by many scholars, though some avoid cards with interest-bearing terms; ask a scholar.\n- Interest your bank puts into your account: take it out and give it away to the poor or for public good, with no intention of reward for yourself (the view of most scholars today). Do not keep it. If you can, move to an interest-free account.\n- If you are already in a loan with interest, you do not tear up the contract, but you repent, pay it off as soon as you can, and avoid new ones.\n\nFor mortgages, see the separate answer. Ask a scholar for your own situation. The Tayyib Finance section of this app lists halal options.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:275, 2:278-279; Sahih Muslim 1598.\n[GO:/finance]',
    topic: 'riba',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The treatment of bank interest (give it away), credit cards paid in full, and existing loans are contemporary-scholar positions; not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'mortgage',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bmortgage\w*\b',
      r'\bhome loans?\b',
      r'\bhouse loans?\b',
      r'\bbuy(?:ing)? (?:a |my )?(?:house|home|property|flat|apartment)\b',
      r'\bhome financing\b',
      r'\bislamic mortgage\b',
      r'\bhalal mortgage\b',
      r'\bhome purchase\b',
    ],
    answer: 'A conventional mortgage charges interest, and interest (riba) is forbidden (Qur\'an 2:275), so most scholars say a conventional mortgage is not allowed.\n\nMuslims living in Western countries are divided. Some contemporary scholars and councils allow a conventional mortgage as an exception when it is a real need (a family home, no halal option available, renting is not a realistic way to house the family), on the principle of necessity in Islamic law. Others say that necessity here is rare, the prohibition is firm, and renting is possible, so a conventional mortgage is not allowed. This is a genuine disagreement among scholars, and I will not pick one for you.\n\nThe widely accepted route is a halal home finance product: a diminishing partnership (musharakah), lease-to-own (ijarah) or cost-plus (murabaha) arrangement from an Islamic finance provider. These are structured so that no loan interest is charged. Ask a scholar to check the contract before you sign.\n\nMeanwhile, saving for a larger deposit or renting a home is always allowed. For your own decision, please talk to a qualified scholar who knows the local options.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:275.\n[GO:/finance]',
    topic: 'mortgage',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The scholars\' disagreement on conventional mortgages for Muslims in the West is described neutrally; flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
