    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:bitcoin|btc|ethereum|eth|crypto\w*|altcoin\w*|nft\w*|dogecoin|token\w*)\b',
      r'\b(?:stock|stocks|share|shares|equities|investing|investment|investments|day trading|forex|trading|options trading|etf|etfs|mutual funds?|index funds?|dividends?)\b.*\b(?:halal|haram|allowed|permissible|islam\w*|sharia|shariah|forbidden)\b',
      r'\b(?:halal|haram|allowed|permissible|islam\w*|sharia|shariah|forbidden)\b.*\b(?:stock|stocks|share|shares|equities|investing|investment|investments|day trading|forex|trading|options|etf|etfs|mutual funds?|index funds?|dividends?)\b',
      r'\b(?:insurance|takaful)\b',
      r'\bshort ?selling\b',
      r'\bmargin\b.*\btrading\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Bitcoin and other crypto: scholars disagree. Some say it is permitted as a commodity or digital asset, subject to the usual rules (no fraud, no interest, no gambling-style speculation). Others say it is not allowed because it has no real backing, is highly uncertain (gharar), and is mostly used for speculation. Because of this real difference, please ask a scholar you trust before you buy; do not buy something you do not understand.\n\nStocks and shares: owning a share in a real business is generally permitted by most contemporary scholars, if the business itself is halal (not alcohol, pork, gambling, interest-based banking, adult entertainment) and the share is not mostly debt-funded or interest-earning. Many halal screening services and Islamic funds exist for this. Day trading, margin trading, short selling, and options are widely seen as speculative or involving interest, and many scholars forbid them.\n\nInsurance: conventional insurance has uncertainty and an element of gambling, and many scholars forbid it, while allowing what the law requires (such as car insurance) because of need. Takaful (cooperative insurance) is the Islamic alternative.\n\nEverything here is general, and these are areas where scholarly opinions differ. Check with a qualified scholar or an Islamic finance advisor for your own money. The Tayyib Finance section may help.\n[GO:/finance]',
    topic: 'investing',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Crypto, stock screening, trading and insurance are contested contemporary issues and are described neutrally; flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'gambling-lottery',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:gambl\w*|lotter\w*|casino\w*|betting|bet|bets|raffle\w*|poker|scratch cards?|sweepstake\w*|maysir|wager\w*|sports betting|loot box\w*)\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:game|games|story|stories)\b.*\b(?:play|ludo|chess)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Gambling is forbidden in Islam. Allah says: "intoxicants, gambling, [sacrificing on] stone alters [to other than Allah], and divining arrows are but defilement from the work of Satan, so avoid it that you may be successful" (Qur\'an 5:90). In another verse, "In them is great sin and [yet, some] benefit for people. But their sin is greater than their benefit" (Qur\'an 2:219).\n\nThis includes casinos, betting on sports, lotteries, raffles where you pay and may win a prize, scratch cards, and games where you pay real money for chance outcomes (including loot boxes, according to many scholars). A prize won through gambling is haram money.\n\nWhat is allowed: a fair prize in a skill contest where the prize is paid by a sponsor (not from the entrants\' fees), and games for fun with no stake. If you have won money by gambling, many scholars say to give it away to the poor without expecting reward, and to repent and stop.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 5:90, 2:219.\n[GO:/finance]',
    topic: 'gambling',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The handling of past winnings is the view of many scholars; not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'pork',
    patterns: <String>[
