    answer: 'Hair colour:\n- Grey or white hair: the Prophet ﷺ told Abu Bakr\'s father, whose hair and beard were white, to change the colour with something but to avoid black (Sahih Muslim 2102). So henna, or a brown or yellow dye, is recommended; black is disliked by most scholars (some scholars allow it for women, or in war).\n- Dyeing hair that is not grey for fashion, in a non-black colour, is allowed for both men and women by most scholars, as long as the dye is not a barrier that stops water from reaching the hair (it must be washable-through in wudu: stains are fine; paint-like coatings are not).\n- Bleaching or highlights: allowed, within modesty, as long as you are not imitating a way of life that Islam forbids.\n\nHair extensions: attaching other people\'s hair is forbidden in the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 5937; most scholars apply it to real human hair, and some extend it to anything attached that looks like your own. Wigs: scholars differ.\n\nCutting hair: allowed for men and women, with modesty. A woman may trim her hair; she should not cut it to imitate men.\n\nBody hair: removing pubic and underarm hair, clipping the nails and trimming the moustache are part of the fitrah, the natural practices (Sahih Muslim 258). Removing hair elsewhere, such as legs or arms, is not forbidden for a woman; men should not imitate women in ways that are specific to them. Shaving the whole head is allowed for a man, and is part of the rites of Hajj and Umrah.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 2102, 258; Sahih al-Bukhari 5937.',
