    answer: 'Scholars have two positions on music, and both rest on evidence:\n- Most classical scholars of the four schools, and many today, say musical instruments (other than the simple hand drum, the duff, at weddings and Eid) are forbidden. They rely on the report that some of the Prophet\'s followers will treat as lawful fornication, silk, wine and musical instruments (Sahih al-Bukhari 5590). Other scholars question the chain of this report, because Bukhari gives it without a full chain.\n- Other scholars, including some classical ones such as Ibn Hazm, and many modern ones, say no clearly authentic text forbids music itself, and that it is allowed when the words and setting are free of what is forbidden.\n\nBoth agree that the duff and singing are allowed at weddings and Eid. The Prophet ﷺ defended the girls singing in A\'ishah\'s house, saying there is an Eid for every people (Sahih al-Bukhari 952).\n\nNasheeds with only the human voice, or with a duff, are accepted by nearly all scholars if the words are clean. Songs about love affairs, drink or immorality are discouraged by both views. This is a real difference, so follow the scholar you trust and avoid anything that distracts you from prayer and the Qur\'an.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 5590, 952.',
