    answer: 'Islam does not allow a private romantic relationship (boyfriend or girlfriend) outside marriage, but it does give a clean path to love and companionship, which is marriage.\n\nWhat the texts say: "And do not approach unlawful sexual intercourse. Indeed, it is ever an immorality and is evil as a way" (Qur\'an 17:32). Note that Allah says do not "approach" it, so the doors that lead to it (private meetings, flirting, physical contact) are avoided. Men and women are told to lower their gaze and guard themselves (Qur\'an 24:30). The Prophet ﷺ also said: Sahih al-Bukhari 3006.\n\nWhat is allowed: meeting a possible spouse with a family member present, asking about each other\'s religion and character, speaking about marriage openly, and then proceeding quickly to engagement and nikah. Many Muslim couples meet through family, community, mosque events or trusted marriage services. Love in marriage is a sign of Allah: "He placed between you affection and mercy" (Qur\'an 30:21).\n\nIf you have already crossed the line, or have a relationship now, do not despair. Stop what is forbidden, turn to Allah in repentance, and if you wish to be together, ask for her or his hand through the family. Allah says: "do not despair of the mercy of Allah" (Qur\'an 39:53). If you want, I can say more about repentance or about finding a spouse.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 17:32, 24:30, 30:21, 39:53; Sahih al-Bukhari 3006.',
    topic: 'dating',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Dating and chaperoned meetings: standard views; not text-checked beyond the cited texts.',
