    answer: 'Honouring parents is one of the greatest duties in Islam, second only to worship of Allah in the Qur\'an: "your Lord has decreed that you not worship except Him, and to parents, good treatment. Whether one or both of them reach old age [while] with you, say not to them [so much as], "uff," and do not repel them but speak to them a noble word" (Qur\'an 17:23). Qur\'an 31:14 reminds that the mother carried the child in weakness upon weakness.\n\nA man asked the Prophet ﷺ who deserves his best company: Sahih al-Bukhari 5971. A man who wanted to join the fighting was told Sunan an-Nasa\'i 3104, hasan sahih per al-Albani. The Prophet ﷺ also said that a person who finds his parents in old age and does not enter Paradise because of them is humbled (Sahih Muslim 2551).\n\nLimits: obedience is for what is good and lawful. If they order a sin, you do not obey, but you stay kind: "do not obey them but accompany them in [this] world with appropriate kindness" (Qur\'an 31:15). That holds even for parents who are not Muslim. Sahih al-Bukhari 7257.\n\nIn practice: visit, call, serve them, make du\'a for them, and care for them in old age at home where you can; if you truly cannot, a good nursing arrangement with frequent visits is a last resort, not a first choice. If a parent is hard on you, patience and gentle words remain your duty, while you protect yourself from real harm. Ask me about a specific situation if you wish.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 17:23, 31:14, 31:15; Sahih al-Bukhari 5971, 7257; Sunan an-Nasa\'i 3104, hasan sahih per al-Albani; Sahih Muslim 2551.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-parents]',
