    answer: 'Children are a trust. Allah says: "O you who have believed, protect yourselves and your families from a Fire" (Qur\'an 66:6), and the Prophet ﷺ showed mercy to children, saying Sahih al-Bukhari 5997.\n\nWorship: parents are told to teach it gradually. Sunan Abu Dawud 494, hasan sahih per al-Albani. Scholars explain the beating as only a light, non-injurious reminder, if at all, and most advise gentleness, patience and example instead. Fasting and hijab are not obligatory until puberty; families begin training earlier in a gentle way, with encouragement and no pressure that harms the child.\n\nFairness: do not favour one child over another in gifts. The Prophet ﷺ asked a man whether he had given the like of the gift to each of his sons (Sahih al-Bukhari 2587). Never kill or harm children from fear of poverty: "do not kill your children for fear of poverty. We provide for them and for you" (Qur\'an 17:31).\n\nHitting a child as a punishment in anger, or in a way that hurts or leaves a mark, is not from the guidance of the Prophet ﷺ. Teach by love, example, du\'a and patience. The For Children section of this app is made for children to learn in a safe way.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 66:6, 17:31; Sahih al-Bukhari 5997, 2587; Sunan Abu Dawud 494, hasan sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-children-deen]',
